x86_64-linux  there is nothing to compare here, by construction: the case includes <new>, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. What the case is about - that `new (p) T[n]` begins at p with no cookie and builds n objects - is checked by the addresses it prints and by the ledger, under run.sh on every machine.
x86_64-windows  there is nothing to compare here, by construction: the case includes <new>, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. What the case is about - that `new (p) T[n]` begins at p with no cookie and builds n objects - is checked by the addresses it prints and by the ledger, under run.sh on every machine.
arm64-darwin  a difference about emission and not about names: cpp11 emits <new>'s inline constructors (std::exception's, bad_alloc's, bad_array_new_length's), the placement operators as the inline functions the header defines them as, its own per-class vector-constructor loop (___cxx1_vec_new_1S), a memset for the value-initialised array and the ledger's abort, where clang inlines or folds every one of them; and clang has an __Unwind_Resume for a cleanup pad round a scope cpp11 sees nothing to unwind in. Every name both compilers emit agrees.
